Title: Nigeria’s Super-Emitter Flares: An Updated Evaluation of Trends and Causes of Natural Gas Wastage
Abstract: The wasteful flaring and venting of gas associated with oil and gas production and processing is a persistent problem, leading to lost taxable income and reduced energy efficiency and access, adverse health impact, and global warming. This paper updates a 2023 report on the gas flaring behaviors of five Nigerian super-emitter offshore flares operated by international oil companies that had revealed very high flaring levels over the period 2012–2022. Updated satellite data up to June 2026 show a mixed picture of recent performance, with some improvement but also evidence of continued excess flaring behavior; three of the five are still classified as active global super-emitters. The paper explores the reasons for continued flaring behaviors, including the inadequate determination of “routine flaring” that allows continuous flaring to sustain operations. It develops a practical way to measure routine flaring when defined as all flaring except for safety purposes. The paper analyses the interrelationships between gas flaring and oil production processes and demonstrates, with quantitative analysis for each flaring location, that existing incentives have not fostered good quality field operations to minimize flaring and optimize oil reserves. In periods of prolonged high flare intensity, oil production declines faster, potentially compromising ultimate oil recovery. Together, the five flaring sites are estimated to have incurred over USD4 billion of lost potential gas export sales due to flaring. The range of observed outcomes shows that with a stable climate for infrastructure investments, including gas monetization projects, and good operational policies, it is possible to significantly reduce gas flaring.
